      "body_html": "<div author=\"@theplantman\" class=\"quote\" source=\"/post/19381/thread\" timestamp=\"1435859371\"><div class=\"quote_body\"><div class=\"quote_avatar_container\"><div class=\"avatar-wrapper avatar_size_quote avatar-275\" title=\"theplantman\"><div><img alt=\"theplantman Avatar\" src=\"//storage.proboards.com/3292456/avatar/tsuVYJdtRJTyLgoNSdoc.png\"/></div></div></div><div class=\"quote_header\"><a href=\"/post/19381/thread\"><abbr class=\"o-timestamp time\" data-timestamp=\"1435859371000\" title=\"Jul 2, 2015 12:49:31 GMT -5\">Jul 2, 2015 12:49:31 GMT -5</abbr></a> <span itemscope=\"\" itemtype=\"http://schema.org/Person\"><a class=\"user-link js-user-link user-275 group-0\" href=\"/user/275\" itemprop=\"url\" title=\"@theplantman\"><span itemprop=\"name\">theplantman</span></a></span> said:</div>That's actually pretty darn cool. If you told me you had basically a leuco without the white, I'd say \"whatever.\" But seeing it is a different story.<br/><br/>And just outta curiosity, can we get a photo of one of the white-topped wherryis?<div class=\"quote_clear\"></div></div></div><br/><br/><br/>Not quite a white topped wherryi, but a leuco x wherryi nevertheless...one that shows a bunch of green coloration too!  Hopefully I'll get one of those very white topped wherryis to shine:<br/><img src=\"https://farm6.staticflickr.com/5782/20634060746_54f725742e_c.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><br/>If you look carefully, there is a little bit of yellow pigments on the top of the lid:<br/><img src=\"https://farm6.staticflickr.com/5669/20667015061_f1baf74252_c.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/>",
